  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The hostess spots Chef Viktor and delivers an important message from a celebrity, and we will listen to them in Russian first; do not worry about catching every single word.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, вас ожидают.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Кто меня ждет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дима тебя ждет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Нагиев здесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, он за своим столом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Что он хочет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Обычно он сначала звонит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он кажется очень взволнованным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Хорошо, я иду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Принеси ему немного кофе.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello.' The first content word means 'hello', and 'hello' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning.' Notice the word order: 'good' comes first, and 'morning' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, вас ожидают.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, they are waiting for you.' Here 'chef' sets up the idea, while 'to expect' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Кто меня ждет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who is waiting for me?' You'll hear 'who' all the time in the show, here connected with 'wait for'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дима тебя ждет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dima is waiting for you.' The key word here means 'Dima', which the speaker combines with 'you'. Then Chef Viktor replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Нагиев здесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nagiyev is here?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'Nagiyev' straight to 'here'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, он за своим столом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, he is at his table.' the speaker puts 'he' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'of yours'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Что он хочет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What does he want?' In Russian, 'what' leads into 'wants' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not know.' The first content word means 'I', and 'know' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Обычно он сначала звонит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He usually calls first.' Listen for the word meaning 'usually' right alongside 'he' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он кажется очень взволнованным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He seems very excited.' Listen for the word meaning 'he' right alongside 'it seems' in this line. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Хорошо, я иду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, I am coming.' A compact expression built around 'good' and 'going'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Принеси ему немного кофе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Bring him some coffee.' In Russian, 'bring' leads into 'of coffee' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
